  :adr/body "## What moved

`cloud.itonami.app.human-work-marketplace/page-html` renders one large HTML
document around an embedded title. Two of its steps are decisions about the
caller-supplied brand name, and both are expressible on the native word-typed
slice (ADR-2608650000 / ADR-2607279200):

- `pick-brand-name` — the cljc's `(or brand-name \"Cloud Itonami\")`. An
  absent name is replaced with the product wordmark; the default is a product
  decision, not a technical fallback.
- `needs-escape?` — whether the chosen name contains any of the three
  characters the cljc's escape map rewrites (`&` `<` `>`).

Landed this tick:

- `src/cloud/itonami/app/human_work_marketplace_core.kotoba` — both exports,
  over `[:option :string]`, gated by `option-some?` (ADR-2609081000's nil
  discipline: a missing name answers wordmark / false, never throws)
- `kotoba_oracle.cljc` `cores` gains `:human-work-marketplace-core`
- `human_work_marketplace.clj` `page-html` now calls the core through
  `kotoba-oracle`; the one-pass `str/escape` substitution stays on the host
- parity test + KIR regeneration + JVM-free gen_kir_amu identity (20/20)

## The substitution stays host-side, and the page is unchanged

The escape substitution itself is a string-mutation operation (a
character-map one-pass replace) that the native slice does not admit — there
is no replacement or concatenation lowering (measured on the pinned verifier:
`str` concat → `:kotoba.error/subset-reject`; `string-contains?` → `:ok
true`, `effects #{}`). So the split is: core decides, host applies, exactly
the ADR-2609081000 pattern. The `if escape-needed` branch is meaningful:
escaping a name containing none of the three characters is the identity, so
the rendered page is byte-identical to the pre-split page for every input.

## Measured bounds on the pinned verifier (amu bin, jvm-free)

- `string-contains?` on `:string` → admitted, `:ok true`, `effects #{}`
  (matches ADR-2609081000's re-measurement; the fleet_core 2026-08-08 note
  remains stale for this pin)
- `if`/`option-value` returning `:string`, and string-literal returns →
  admitted
- `str` concatenation → refused (`:kotoba.error/subset-reject`)

